# The declared development stack's configuration. Compose reads this file on
# its own, and so does the test harness.
#
# Every value here is a development constant on purpose: this stack is meant
# to be thrown away and recreated, and a secret worth protecting has no
# business being reproducible from a file in a public repository. There is no
# such secret yet. When the first one arrives — the Google and GitHub client
# secrets — it is referenced and not declared, which means a name here and a
# value in an untracked file. That mechanism is not built yet, so nothing in
# this repository reads one today.

# The Broker's issuer, declared once and read from here by everything that
# needs it (ADR-0001). It is never persisted in any database and it is never
# reconstructed by concatenating parts: this string is the configuration, and
# what Keycloak is told is derived from it rather than assembled beside it.
#
# `/realms/<name>` stays in the path on purpose (ADR-0001). The name has to
# match the realm this repository declares in broker/realm-citsci.json, and
# the discovery case says so out loud when it does not.
#
# One name on both sides of the port mapping. `broker.localhost` is the
# loopback address for a browser (RFC 6761, honoured by macOS and by every
# browser) and, through a network alias on the reverse proxy, this stack from
# inside the compose network. Pinning it to `localhost` instead would leave
# anything running in a container unable to reach the issuer it is told to
# trust, because localhost inside a container is the container.
#
# The one trap, measured in the earlier effort: musl resolves anything under
# .localhost to the loopback itself and never asks Docker's DNS, so an
# Alpine-based Federated App container would find itself rather than the
# Broker. If some image ever has to be musl, the fallback is a name outside
# .localhost — broker.federation.test resolves under both libcs — plus one
# /etc/hosts line on the host for the browser.
BROKER_ISSUER=http://broker.localhost:8080/realms/citsci
